---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    updatedDate={updatedDate}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    category={category}
    categorySlug={categorySlug}
    subcategorySlug={subcategorySlug}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Tested desktop speakers for fifty-ish hours in my actual home office. Audioengine A2+ won. Clear calls, good music,
    fits any desk.
  </p>

  <p>
    Laptop speakers are terrible. We all know this. Desktop speakers fix that. Better dialogue on video calls. Music
    that doesn't sound like it's coming from a tin can. Your ears don't hurt after eight hours.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-picks">Quick Picks: Our Top 3</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={quickPicksColumns} rows={quickPicksRows}/>

  <h2 id="why-desktop-speakers">Why Bother</h2>

  <p>
    People skip audio when setting up home offices. Mistake. You're at your computer eight to ten hours daily. Audio
    matters.
  </p>

  <h3>What's better than laptop speakers</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Actually hearing what people say on calls</li>
    <li>No ear pressure since you're not wearing headphones</li>
    <li>Stereo separation for music</li>
    <li>Can turn things up without distortion</li>
    <li>More engaged on calls when you hear clearly</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-to-look-for">What Matters</h2>

  <h3>Audio quality</h3>
  <p>
    Focus on midrange clarity, not bass. Voices should sound normal. Music should have separation. Nothing that sounds
    harsh when you turn it up.
  </p>

  <h3>Connections</h3>
  <p>
    USB and Bluetooth are what you actually use. USB is simple. Bluetooth lets you switch to phone quickly. Some do
    both. That's the move.
  </p>

  <h3>Size</h3>
  <p>
    Desk space is limited. Under six inches tall works for most setups. Angled designs point sound at your ears instead
    of your chest.
  </p>

  <h3>Build</h3>
  <p>
    Good ones last years. Solid brands. Plastic is fine for cheap stuff. Nicer ones use wood or metal.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audioengine-a2-plus">Audioengine A2+</h2>

  <p>
    My top pick. These have earned their reputation. Small but powerful. Audio that sounds like it costs more than it
    does.
  </p>

  <h3>What makes them good</h3>
  <p>
    Voice clarity mainly. Calls sound natural. Podcasts sound engaged. Music has detail you miss otherwise. Sixty watts
    means headroom.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Custom drivers and silk tweeters</li>
    <li>USB DAC built in</li>
    <li>aptX Bluetooth</li>
    <li>Subwoofer outputs if you want one later</li>
    <li>Actual wood cabinets available</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    People who spend forty-plus hours at their desk and care about audio. If music matters to you, these are worth it.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/audioengine-a2-plus" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="edifier-r1280db">Edifier R1280DB</h2>

  <p>
    These sound great for the money. Bookshelf style. Warm and balanced.
  </p>

  <h3>Why they're good</h3>
  <p>
    Edifier figured out affordable speakers. Forty-two watts. Bluetooth, optical, remote control included. Other
    speakers at this price don't have all that.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Four inch bass drivers</li>
    <li>Bluetooth 5.0, optical, dual RCA</li>
    <li>Wireless remote</li>
    <li>Bass and treble controls on the side</li>
    <li>Wood finish</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    People who play music while working. Works with everything. That hi-fi feeling without spending hi-fi money.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/edifier-r1280db" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="creative-pebble-v3">Creative Pebble V3</h2>

  <p>
    Thirty-nine bucks. Don't laugh. These are actually good. Compact. Clear. Way better than laptop speakers.
  </p>

  <h3>Why they're good</h3>
  <p>
    For under forty dollars you get USB-C power, Bluetooth, and sound that makes more expensive speakers look bad.
    Drivers tilt up at forty-five degrees so they point at you.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>USB-C with Bluetooth</li>
    <li>Upward-firing drivers</li>
    <li>Gain switch for more volume</li>
    <li>Tiny footprint</li>
    <li>Eight watts. Plenty for one person</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    Tight budgets. Small desks. Anyone who wants better than laptop speakers without spending much.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/creative-pebble-v3" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="logitech-z407">Logitech Z407</h2>

  <p>
    This one has a subwoofer. Full sound. Wireless dial for volume is nice.
  </p>

  <h3>Why it's good</h3>
  <p>
    Bass without complexity. Subwoofer adds depth for music and videos. The small speakers handle everything else.
    Bluetooth, USB, aux. Covers your devices.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Forty watts with subwoofer</li>
    <li>Multiple connection types</li>
    <li>Wireless dial controller</li>
    <li>Twenty meter Bluetooth range</li>
    <li>Put the sub anywhere</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    Home offices that double as entertainment spaces. YouTube, streaming, games during breaks.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/logitech-z407" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bose-companion-2">Bose Companion 2 III</h2>

  <p>
    Bose sound in a small package. Focused on clarity.
  </p>

  <h3>Why they're good</h3>
  <p>
    Bose does audio well. TrueSpace processing makes these sound wider than they should. Voices sound present. Great for
    calls.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>TrueSpace processing</li>
    <li>Volume and headphone controls</li>
    <li>Two inputs at once</li>
    <li>Clean design</li>
    <li>Doesn't waste power</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    Bose fans. People who want clear voices over bass. Calls, podcasts, audiobooks.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/bose-companion-2" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="klipsch-promedia">Klipsch ProMedia 2.1</h2>

  <p>
    THX-certified. Serious desktop audio. Nothing under two hundred bucks competes.
  </p>

  <h3>Why they're good</h3>
  <p>
    Two hundred watts. Horn tweeters that Klipsch is known for. Movies, games, music. All sound great. Big six-and-a-half
    inch subwoofer.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>THX certified</li>
    <li>Horn tweeters</li>
    <li>Big subwoofer</li>
    <li>Lots of power</li>
    <li>Headphone jack</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    Gamers and movie people. Way more than you need for quiet work but perfect when work is done.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/klipsch-promedia" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="edifier-r1280t">Edifier R1280T</h2>

  <p>
    Classic bookshelf look. Modern sound. Good on any desk.
  </p>

  <h3>Why they're good</h3>
  <p>
    Ninety-nine bucks. Wood cabinets look expensive. Sound quality beats speakers at double the price. Two RCA inputs
    for computer and phone.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Forty-two watts</li>
    <li>Four inch drivers</li>
    <li>Dual inputs</li>
    <li>Bass and treble knobs</li>
    <li>Remote included</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    Design people. These look good with mid-century modern stuff. Traditional offices too.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/edifier-r1280t" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="razer-nommo">Razer Nommo</h2>

  <p>
    Gaming brand but these work anywhere. Cylindrical shape gets more bass from less space.
  </p>

  <h3>Why they're good</h3>
  <p>
    Despite being Razer, sound is balanced for actual work. Bass ports in back. Glass fiber drivers sound full.
  </p>

  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Three inch glass fiber drivers</li>
    <li>Rear bass ports</li>
    <li>USB and 3.5mm</li>
    <li>Bass dial on the speaker</li>
    <li>Auto gain control</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best for</h3>
  <p>
    Remote workers who also game. Modern look. Works for both work and play.
  </p>

  <div class="my-8">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/razer-nommo" text="Check Price on Amazon"/>
  </div>

  <h2 id="comparison-table">Full Comparison Table</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows}/>

  <h2 id="speakers-vs-headphones">Speakers vs Headphones</h2>

  <p>
    No clear winner. Different tools.
  </p>

  <h3>Speakers work for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Long sessions without fatigue</li>
    <li>Background music</li>
    <li>Hearing your environment</li>
    <li>Casual listening</li>
    <li>When you're alone</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Headphones work for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Video calls, no echo</li>
    <li>Deep focus needing isolation</li>
    <li>Shared spaces</li>
    <li>Late nights</li>
    <li>Private content</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Best setup</h3>
  <p>
    Most productive people have both. Speakers for general stuff. Headphones for calls and focus. Many speakers have
    headphone jacks anyway.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqItems}/>

  <h2>Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>
    Desktop speakers improve the home office. Most people: get the A2+. Tight budget: Pebble V3 proves cheap can sound
    good.
  </p>

  <p>
    Think about your space and budget. Music people like Edifier. Entertainment people check Klipsch or Logitech.
    Whatever works for you, good speakers make work days better.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
